--- a/pod/perlfunc.pod
+++ b/pod/perlfunc.pod
@@ -5127,6 +5127,21 @@
 
     print STDOUT "stdout 2\n";
     print STDERR "stderr 2\n";
+
+Re-opening a filehandle over a dup normally gives it the PerlIO layer
+stack of the handle being duped.  The standard filehandles are an
+exception: when the handle being re-opened is on file descriptor 0, 1
+or 2, Perl keeps that handle's existing PerlIO object and dups the new
+descriptor underneath it, so its layers stay as they were rather than
+becoming the ones this C<open> would have given it.  This matters for
+the save-and-restore pattern above.  A layer pushed while the handle is
+redirected, say C<binmode STDOUT, ':encoding(utf8)'>, is still in place
+after the restore, and because pushing C<:encoding> does not replace an
+existing one, running the cycle repeatedly stacks one more layer each
+time; pop it with C<binmode STDOUT, ':pop'> before restoring.
+Conversely, where a handle's input and output sides are separate PerlIO
+objects, as they are for a terminal, the re-open discards the layers on
+the output side.
 
 If you specify C<< '<&=X' >>, where C<X> is a file descriptor number
 or a filehandle, then Perl will do an equivalent of C's L<fdopen(3)> of
